1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:19,728
This lesson follows the teaching themes of school 1B: foundations,
communication, partnership, cultural humility and trauma-informed
practice. Our scenarios and reflection exercises are original
preparations for those skills, not reproductions of the school
competency checks or their answers.

2
00:00:19,728 --> 00:00:31,344
The central question is not whether the clinician intended respect. It
is whether the patient experienced dignity, choice, understanding and
safety while receiving needed care.

3
00:00:31,344 --> 00:00:42,456
Cultural humility is an ongoing practice of self-reflection, listening
and learning. It is not a badge a clinician earns or a claim to
understand another person completely.

4
00:00:42,456 --> 00:00:56,928
Cultural safety focuses on the person receiving care and on power.
Equity asks what unequal barriers need to change. Anti-racism requires
action when racism is interpersonal or built into a process.

5
00:00:56,928 --> 00:01:12,168
The school contrasts sympathy with empathy. Sympathy expresses concern
for someone; empathy tries to understand their perspective. Concern is
not automatically harmful, but pity can make the person feel reduced
to a problem.

6
00:01:12,168 --> 00:01:23,520
You cannot know exactly how another person feels. Say what you noticed
and invite correction: You seem worried about leaving your belongings.
Is that part of what is making this difficult?

7
00:01:23,520 --> 00:01:38,856
Compassion adds an appropriate action. With the patient’s agreement,
help identify how important belongings could remain secure while
assessment continues. Do not promise storage, transport or
reimbursement that you cannot arrange.

8
00:01:38,856 --> 00:01:50,856
Avoid comparing the patient with your own experience or immediately
correcting their emotion. The learning target is observable listening
and useful support, not performing a reassuring speech.

9
00:01:50,856 --> 00:02:06,432
A uniform, stretcher, police presence, pain, language difference or
previous harmful health-care experience can make an ordinary question
feel coercive. The patient may have less control over movement,
privacy and pace.

10
00:02:06,432 --> 00:02:21,288
Before interpreting hesitation as non-cooperation, ask what barrier,
fear, symptom or prior experience could explain it. Reflection does
not delay emergency care; it improves how that care is offered.

11
00:02:21,288 --> 00:02:32,880
AVS is the school’s communication prompt: acknowledge, validate and
support. Use it as a flexible sequence, not a script that every
patient must accept.

12
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:45,288
An original response might be: You are concerned that nobody will hear
your request. Being overlooked would be frustrating. I can listen to
the main concern first and then explain what our assessment can offer.

13
00:02:45,288 --> 00:02:58,536
Validating distress does not mean confirming a false accusation,
agreeing with a delusion or accepting a threat. You can recognize fear
while remaining honest about what you know and what conduct is safe.

14
00:02:58,536 --> 00:03:13,680
Check the effect. If tension rises, reduce demands, reconsider
distance and seek support as indicated. Communication can improve an
encounter; it never guarantees cooperation or removes a real hazard.

15
00:03:13,680 --> 00:03:24,720
Equal treatment gives everyone the same step. Equitable care asks
whether the same step is actually accessible and whether an avoidable
barrier is creating a different outcome.

16
00:03:24,720 --> 00:03:39,600
Social determinants of health include conditions such as housing,
income, education, food security, social support and access to care.
They shape risk and choices, but they do not define a person or
predict what they value.

17
00:03:39,600 --> 00:03:51,864
The school uses a five-domain organizer for social determinants. Learn
those five names for this module, but do not mistake this teaching
model for the only Canadian classification of health determinants.

18
00:03:51,864 --> 00:04:08,208
Economic stability concerns resources for necessities. Health-care
access and quality concern obtaining suitable services. A patient may
have provincial coverage yet still face a transport, appointment,
medication-cost or communication barrier.

19
00:04:08,208 --> 00:04:24,576
Education access and quality concern learning opportunities and usable
information. Do not infer intelligence or understanding from
schooling: pain, unfamiliar terminology, hearing, language and stress
can affect anyone’s comprehension.

20
00:04:24,576 --> 00:04:42,168
Neighbourhood and built environment include housing, transport and
local conditions. Social and community context include relationships,
inclusion and support. These domains interact; use them to discover a
barrier, not to diagnose a person’s character.

21
00:04:42,168 --> 00:04:53,712
Intersectionality is a way to notice how more than one social position
or system can shape a person’s options at the same time. It is a
question prompt, not a label to apply to someone.

22
00:04:53,712 --> 00:05:07,176
A patient with limited mobility who lives far from services and has no
phone may face a different follow-up barrier than a patient with the
same diagnosis and reliable transport. Ask what is true for this
person today.

23
00:05:07,176 --> 00:05:23,088
In an original case, a patient says the clinic’s text-message
instructions are unusable. Do not chart refusal or lack of motivation
before finding out why. They may have a dead device, low vision, a
language need or no private access.

24
00:05:23,088 --> 00:05:37,608
Ask one relevant question at a time: Which part prevents you using
this plan? Ask permission before involving another person or viewing
private messages. Do not collect unrelated financial or immigration
details.

25
00:05:37,608 --> 00:05:51,912
Offer feasible options through approved pathways, such as a clearer
explanation or a suitable communication format. A support person may
help if the patient wants them involved. Do not invent a referral
service or guarantee an appointment.

26
00:05:51,912 --> 00:06:04,776
Close with the actual plan and the unresolved barrier. An equitable
response is not merely identifying disadvantage; it is making a useful
adjustment and checking whether the patient can use it.

27
00:06:04,776 --> 00:06:18,432
A therapeutic relationship is professional, purposeful and time-
limited. Warmth supports care, but it does not remove consent,
confidentiality, scope or documentation responsibilities.

28
00:06:18,432 --> 00:06:31,368
Introduce your role, explain what you would like to do, ask
permission, and make space for questions. If urgent care limits a
choice, state the reason plainly and offer the choices that remain.

29
00:06:31,368 --> 00:07:00,672
The school groups a therapeutic environment under five pillars:
comfort, dignity, privacy, engagement and safety. Comfort asks what
physical or sensory burden can be reduced. Dignity concerns respectful
treatment and identity. Privacy concerns exposure and information.
Engagement means the patient participates rather than being talked
over. Safety includes the patient and crew, not merely a quiet room.

30
00:07:00,672 --> 00:07:29,784
Apply all five to the community-centre encounter. Ask whether a warmer
or less noisy position would help; use the patient’s preferred name;
reduce unnecessary exposure and overhearing; invite their main concern
and check your explanation. Before moving, confirm safe access,
observation and exits with your partner. An adjustment can support
several pillars, but must not sacrifice urgent care to complete a
comfort checklist.

31
00:07:29,784 --> 00:08:06,576
Privacy is practical: explain the measurement and contact, ask
permission, expose only the required area, and shield the patient from
unnecessary observers. Ask how they wish a support person to
participate rather than treating that person as the automatic
decision-maker. Speak where others cannot unnecessarily overhear and
protect records. A curtain protects visual privacy but does not make a
conversation inaudible. Necessary communication for care should be
accurate and appropriately limited, not omitted in the name of
privacy.

32
00:08:06,576 --> 00:08:40,848
The book mentions fifteen seconds for hand gel and washing. Keep the
methods separate: current CDC clinical guidance emphasizes enough
alcohol hand rub to cover every surface and rubbing until dry, usually
about twenty seconds. Its soap-and-water guidance allows at least
fifteen seconds and also recognizes twenty-second recommendations. Do
not call every fifteen-second wash wrong, or stop rubbing a still-wet
product just because a timer rang. Follow the actual product and local
infection-control method.

33
00:08:40,848 --> 00:08:52,968
Do not infer language, beliefs, family roles, pain expression, eye
contact or touch preferences from appearance or group membership. Ask
the individual and confirm before acting.

34
00:08:52,968 --> 00:09:07,200
Use a qualified interpreter when available and appropriate. Speak to
the patient, use short direct sentences, pause for interpretation, and
avoid asking children to interpret sensitive or high-stakes
information.

35
00:09:07,200 --> 00:09:18,120
Replace jargon with a short explanation. Instead of only saying that
we need a baseline, explain that you want to learn what is normal for
this person so that a new change is not missed.

36
00:09:18,120 --> 00:09:31,776
After one important idea, ask an open question about the action: To
check that I explained the next step clearly, how will you tell the
receiving team what changed today? A yes to Do you understand is
weaker evidence.

37
00:09:31,776 --> 00:09:46,512
If the explanation comes back differently, take responsibility for
clarifying. Rephrase, demonstrate or use appropriate interpretation;
then ask again without blame. Repeating the same jargon louder is not
a repair.

38
00:09:46,512 --> 00:10:00,768
Teach-back supports communication. It is not a stand-alone capacity
test or a demand that an exhausted patient perform a lesson before
receiving urgent care. Match the check to the person and the immediate
clinical situation.

39
00:10:00,768 --> 00:10:31,752
The opening page also shows OARS from motivational interviewing: open
questions, affirmations, reflections and summaries. Begin with an
invitation rather than a presumed answer; acknowledge a genuine effort
or strength, reflect the meaning tentatively, then summarize and
invite correction. This is not praise for obedience or a technique for
making someone choose your preference. Use focused closed questions
too when an urgent fact needs a precise answer.

40
00:10:31,752 --> 00:11:03,792
Evaluate communication as a feedback loop: set a purpose, speak or
listen, observe the response, check the intended meaning, and revise.
If the person repeats a concern you thought was settled, ask what
remains unclear rather than repeating your explanation louder. Teach-
back can check whether your explanation was usable. Success means
understanding and a safer, workable interaction; agreement, calmness
or acceptance of transport alone does not prove it.

41
00:11:03,792 --> 00:11:20,448
Ask which language or communication method works best; do not select
it from the person’s name or appearance. Use the approved professional
language service and explain its role. Spoken-language interpretation
and sign-language access are not the same request.

42
00:11:20,448 --> 00:11:34,728
Speak to the patient rather than asking the interpreter to tell them
everything. Use one short, complete thought, then pause. Avoid side
conversations that exclude the patient or several questions bundled
into one turn.

43
00:11:34,728 --> 00:11:52,488
Ask the patient how a chosen support person should participate.
Support and professional interpretation have different roles. A
relative may provide history with permission, but that does not
establish accurate, private interpretation of consent or sensitive
concerns.

44
00:11:52,488 --> 00:12:09,600
Check understanding through interpretation and document the mode used
and material limits. If communication cannot be established during an
urgent problem, use current emergency procedures and consultation; do
not fabricate agreement or a fluent conversation.

45
00:12:09,600 --> 00:12:23,256
Body position, eye contact, silence and proximity can carry different
meanings for different people. A single cue does not establish
hostility, consent, deception or a psychiatric condition.

46
00:12:23,256 --> 00:12:36,744
Describe what you observed without judgment, then ask. A check-in
protects the relationship and may reveal pain, fear, sensory overload,
a need for more space or simply a personal preference.

47
00:12:36,744 --> 00:12:51,096
Approach at a pace that permits the person to notice you. Introduce
movement before reaching toward equipment or the patient. Visible
hands and a relaxed posture can reduce uncertainty, but their meaning
must still be checked.

48
00:12:51,096 --> 00:13:06,312
If safe, avoid towering over a seated person. Use a non-
confrontational angle and appropriate distance while keeping a clear
exit. Do not crouch into an unsafe position, block the patient’s route
or let a crowd surround them.

49
00:13:06,312 --> 00:13:22,176
Reduce noise, unnecessary personnel and exposure where feasible. Ask
before changing lighting or moving a support person. A quiet room is
helpful only if access, observation and clinical care remain safe.

50
00:13:22,176 --> 00:13:37,200
Silence, eye contact and personal space do not have one universal
meaning. Ask whether your position works for this person and adapt to
hearing, vision, mobility and expressed preferences rather than
demanding a performance of respect.

51
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:49,728
Trauma-informed practice recognizes that distress may be linked to
previous or current harm. It does not require the patient to tell a
trauma story, and it does not diagnose trauma from behavior.

52
00:13:49,728 --> 00:14:01,128
Predictability can help: announce movement before touching, explain
the monitor, offer a less exposed position, and let the person choose
which safe arm is examined first.

53
00:14:01,128 --> 00:14:14,616
A raised voice or fearful expression does not by itself establish
violence. Describe behaviour and context rather than labeling the
person aggressive because of a cultural difference, frustration or a
refusal.

54
00:14:14,616 --> 00:14:31,320
Specific threatening actions, a weapon or rapidly worsening behaviour
can change scene safety. Maintain a route away, coordinate with your
partner and request the appropriate resources. A compassionate
approach does not require staying in an uncontrolled threat.

55
00:14:31,320 --> 00:14:46,824
BCEHS G01 also directs attention to medical contributors such as
hypoxia, hypoglycemia, head injury, drug effects or infection. Do not
let a psychiatric label or a conflict distract from assessment of a
new clinical change.

56
00:14:46,824 --> 00:15:02,664
Continue safe communication while assessment and support are arranged.
This lesson does not grant detention, restraint or medication
authority. Those decisions need the current legal and service pathway,
not a communication mnemonic.

57
00:15:02,664 --> 00:15:18,816
At a fictional community-centre entrance, several people answer over a
patient who asks everyone to back away. If safe, stop advancing,
create space and arrange one clinician to speak. Do not begin by
demanding that the patient calm down.

58
00:15:18,816 --> 00:15:31,080
Try: I will stay here while you tell me the main concern. What would
you like us to hear first? Then pause long enough for an answer.
Acknowledge the concern before moving to the next assessment question.

59
00:15:31,080 --> 00:15:45,216
Offer a genuine small choice: We can explain the check here, or in
that quieter area if it is safe to move. Do not offer a choice that
has already been decided, and do not bargain away necessary safety
controls.

60
00:15:45,216 --> 00:16:01,248
If the person says no, clarify what was declined and reassess the
situation. If distress or danger rises, change the plan and obtain
support. A failed first phrase is information, not a reason to become
punitive.

61
00:16:01,248 --> 00:16:14,928
If a patient reports discriminatory treatment, do not debate whether
the experience “counts.” Listen, acknowledge the concern, clarify what
the person needs now and take a practical step within your role.

62
00:16:14,928 --> 00:16:29,760
If a colleague’s conduct creates immediate risk, prioritize patient
safety, speak up through the appropriate chain and document objective
events under service policy. Do not promise confidentiality or a
particular investigation result.

63
00:16:29,760 --> 00:16:46,032
The school and FNHA frame Indigenous cultural safety in the context of
colonial harms, racism and unequal power in care. This context helps
explain why a service must earn trust rather than expect automatic
trust in a uniform.

64
00:16:46,032 --> 00:17:00,768
Do not assume that every Indigenous patient has the same history,
beliefs, family structure or preferred support. First Nations, Inuit
and Metis identities are distinct, and individual preferences still
need to be asked.

65
00:17:00,768 --> 00:17:14,784
Ask whether there is someone or something important to include in the
present care, without requiring a trauma narrative or proof of
identity. Explore feasible patient-chosen support and explain clinical
limits transparently.

66
00:17:14,784 --> 00:17:31,368
If a discriminatory assumption is diverting attention from symptoms,
redirect toward the patient’s actual findings and needs. Support the
affected person and use the appropriate accountability route. Good
intentions do not certify that the interaction felt safe.

67
00:17:31,368 --> 00:18:02,352
School 1B names residential schools, the Sixties Scoop, Indian
hospitals and the In Plain Sight review. Residential schools separated
children from family and suppressed culture. The Sixties Scoop
involved removal of Indigenous children into non-Indigenous care.
Indian hospitals were part of segregated health care. In Plain Sight
examines Indigenous-specific racism in British Columbia health care.
These are distinct subjects, not interchangeable names for one event.

68
00:18:02,352 --> 00:18:32,064
Use that context to examine the power and assumptions in your own
encounter. It does not prove that this patient experienced a
particular event or explains their symptoms. In the existing guarded-
patient case, ask what would help now, preserve the medical assessment
and offer patient-chosen support when feasible. Challenge a
discriminatory shortcut with actual findings, and use the appropriate
accountability route. Do not require the patient to disclose trauma or
teach you their history.

69
00:18:32,064 --> 00:18:45,144
If you mispronounce a name, expose someone without warning or dismiss
a concern, a brief repair can reduce additional harm. Avoid centering
your intent or asking the patient to reassure you.

70
00:18:45,144 --> 00:18:57,456
Accountability includes follow-through. If the person requests a
different clinician, interpreter, support person or complaint route,
explore what is feasible and explain any limit honestly.

71
00:18:57,456 --> 00:19:12,888
The school uses PACE for speaking up with a colleague: probe, alert,
challenge and emergency. It gives increasing clarity when a softer
message is not heard; it does not require you to wait through four
stages during imminent harm.

72
00:19:12,888 --> 00:19:25,944
An original probe is: Would one speaker and more space help this
interview? An alert adds the effect: Our repeated interruptions are
increasing distress and preventing us hearing the symptoms.

73
00:19:25,944 --> 00:19:41,136
A challenge is direct: Stop using that label; describe the finding we
actually assessed. If immediate harm is developing, use the safe
intervention, supervisor or emergency support pathway appropriate to
your role and the scene.

74
00:19:41,136 --> 00:19:55,296
Speaking up is not permission to physically confront a colleague or
abandon another patient. Preserve care, communicate the changed
assignment and document relevant facts through the correct process
after the immediate problem is controlled.

75
00:19:55,296 --> 00:20:08,544
A patient with new confusion after a fall says, “No one touches me.” A
family member asks you to force an examination. The patient is alert
but guarded; no immediate airway threat is visible.

76
00:20:08,544 --> 00:20:25,200
Do not infer incapacity from the refusal or appearance. Explain the
concern, ask permission to talk, offer privacy and choices, use the
communication support the patient prefers, and assess whether an
emergency response or consultation is required.

77
00:20:25,200 --> 00:20:38,136
A useful reflection names a behavior, its impact and the next change.
“I was culturally sensitive” is not observable; “I asked how the
patient wanted the family involved” can be observed and improved.

78
00:20:38,136 --> 00:20:49,272
These prompts prepare for class discussion, not a self-certification.
Practice with an instructor and request feedback from the person
receiving care whenever it is appropriate and safe.

79
00:20:49,272 --> 00:21:03,144
Anchoring means an early explanation holds too strongly. If a dispatch
label says intoxication, a new focal weakness must still change your
assessment. The initial label is not the patient’s diagnosis.

80
00:21:03,144 --> 00:21:16,920
Confirmation bias means favouring information that supports what you
already think. Ask what finding would make the explanation wrong, then
look for it through the appropriate assessment rather than collecting
only agreeing details.

81
00:21:16,920 --> 00:21:31,992
Attribution bias can turn a missed appointment into a judgment about
character. Ask about the actual access problem. A transport barrier
and a lack of concern are different explanations; neither should be
invented without evidence.

82
00:21:31,992 --> 00:21:46,800
For class rehearsal, an observer should hear the question, see the
adjustment and identify the reassessment. Reflection is useful when it
changes the next behaviour; merely saying I have no bias does not
demonstrate a skill.
